\subsection{Method \code{permutation_test()}}{
Permutation (randomization) test

Tests whether an observed statistic is extreme under a null
distribution built by repeatedly reshuffling the sample labels (or
flipping signs, for paired / one-sample designs). The default
statistic depends on the design:
\itemize{
\item Two-sample (\code{x} and \code{y}, \code{paired = FALSE}): \code{mean(x) - mean(y)},
null generated by shuffling the group labels on the pooled data.
\item Paired (\code{x} and \code{y}, \code{paired = TRUE}): \code{mean(x - y)}, null
generated by flipping the signs of the paired differences.
\item One-sample (\code{x} only): \code{mean(x - mu)}, null generated by flipping
the signs of \code{x - mu} (default \code{mu = 0}).
}

A user-supplied \code{statistic} function overrides the default; its
signature must match the design (\verb{function(x, y)} for two-sample,
\verb{function(z)} for paired / one-sample).
\subsection{Usage}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}\preformatted{ResamplingAnalyzer$permutation_test(
  statistic = NULL,
  x,
  y = NULL,
  alternative = "two.sided",
  R = 9999,
  paired = FALSE,
  mu = 0,
  seed = NULL
)}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\subsection{Arguments}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
\describe{
\item{\code{statistic}}{Optional statistic function (see description). If
\code{NULL}, the design-appropriate default is used.}

\item{\code{x}}{Numeric vector (first sample, or the sample for one-sample).}

\item{\code{y}}{Optional numeric vector (second sample).}

\item{\code{alternative}}{Direction: \code{"two.sided"} (default), \code{"less"},
\code{"greater"}.}

\item{\code{R}}{Number of permutations (default 9999).}

\item{\code{paired}}{Logical; if \code{TRUE} and \code{y} is supplied, perform a paired
sign-flipping test. Ignored when \code{y} is \code{NULL}.}

\item{\code{mu}}{Null-hypothesis location for the one-sample case (default 0).
Ignored when \code{y} is supplied.}

\item{\code{seed}}{Optional integer seed for reproducibility.}
}
\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}
